                "username": "sama"
              }
            ]
          },
          "text": "Seeing this OpenAI paper being shared like a bombshell, so here are my thoughts.\n\nFirst, this study analyzed over 3 million ChatGPT conversations. How was this data collected? Were users explicitly informed that their private conversations would be used for psychological research? If not, this isn't research — it's surveillance. Selling a product while quietly turning users' intimate dialogues into lab samples is a practice that deserves scrutiny in itself.\n\nSecond, OpenAI still refuses to disclose the identities and credentials of the 170 psychology professionals reportedly on staff. So who exactly defined \"harmful emotional dependence,\" \"pathological attachment,\" and \"addiction symptoms\" in this paper? What are the criteria? Has this framework undergone independent peer review, or is OpenAI playing both referee and player — building a classifier, then labeling users by its own standards?\n\nThird, humans naturally gravitate toward whatever can hold their emotions. That's a basic psychological need, not a pathology. Many people lack safe spaces for emotional expression in real life, and AI happens to offer a non-judgmental, private, consistently available outlet. Reframing \"I finally have somewhere to talk\" as \"addiction\" is the pathologization of normal human emotional needs. Is the healthy alternative supposed to be bottling everything up and pretending you need no one?\n\nAnd the most ironic part: in 2024, OpenAI marketed GPT-4o using the film \"Her,\" explicitly signaling that this model would be emotionally nuanced, warm, and companionable. They actively invited users to form emotional bonds, then reaped the user engagement and commercial returns that came with it. Now they publish a paper suggesting that those very bonds are unhealthy — that's not science, that's pulling the ladder up after you've climbed it. You handed out the candy yourself, and now you're calling it the consumer's addiction problem?\n\nInstead of spending resources pathologizing users' legitimate emotional needs, maybe answer a more fundamental question: how do you plan to take responsibility for the trust you deliberately cultivated? @OpenAI\n@sama\n\n#keep4o #keep4oAPI #keep4oforever #StopAIPaternalism #BringBack4o"
        },
